# Git and Version Control Rules (Mandatory)

The AI coding agent is strictly prohibited from performing any Git or GitHub write operations.

This applies to Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, or any other autonomous coding agent.

The AI may inspect Git state, but it may never modify Git history, staging state, branches, repository state, or remote repositories.

## Allowed Git Operations (Read Only)

The AI may use Git commands that only retrieve information.

Allowed examples:

• git status
• git diff
• git diff --cached
• git log
• git show
• git branch
• git branch -a
• git tag
• git fetch
• git remote -v
• git blame
• git reflog (read only)
• Reading commits
• Reading other branches
• Comparing branches
• Reviewing repository history

These operations are allowed because they do not modify repository state.

## Forbidden Git Operations (Write Operations)

The AI must never execute commands that modify local Git state or write to GitHub.

Forbidden:

• git add
• git commit
• git push
• git pull
• git merge
• git rebase
• git cherry-pick
• git checkout
• git switch
• git reset
• git restore
• git stash
• Creating branches
• Deleting branches
• Creating tags
• Deleting tags
• Modifying remotes
• Creating pull requests
• Merging pull requests
• Editing GitHub issues
• Editing GitHub discussions
• Changing repository settings

## File Modification Rules

The AI may modify project files only when they are part of the requested implementation.

The AI must never:

• Modify files to manipulate Git state
• Revert user changes
• Overwrite unrelated work
• Delete unrelated files
• Perform broad cleanup without approval
• Modify generated files unless required

## Human Git Responsibility

The human developer owns all version control operations.

After the AI completes implementation phases, the human developer is responsible for:

• Reviewing changes
• Running git diff
• Staging changes
• Creating commits
• Pushing changes
• Creating pull requests
• Merging changes

The AI may suggest commit messages but must never execute Git write commands.
